The MessagePasser class provides a simple, in-memory message queue for agent communication—no external APIs or paid services required.
Both research and coding agents can send and receive structured messages, supporting extensible message types (e.g., research-results, code-response).
The CommunicationMonitor gives you visibility into the message flow, which is great for debugging and transparency.
The main.js orchestrates the system, ensuring agents interact and messages are processed in real time.
This pattern is lightweight, extensible, and perfectly suited for multi-agent experiments on a free tier or local setup. It also lays the groundwork for more advanced features (e.g., message persistence, agent registration, or distributed operation) if you ever want to expand.

If you’d like, I can help you:

Integrate this pattern into your existing codebase
Add message persistence (e.g., to disk, for crash recovery)
Extend the protocol for more agent types or richer message types
Document this system in your repo for future contributors
Just let me know what you’d like to do next!

